1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,340
What does a software team actually produce?

2
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:08,540
Code, surely. Piles of it.

3
00:00:08,710 --> 00:00:12,370
In 1985, Peter Naur's answer was not the code.

4
00:00:12,770 --> 00:00:15,170
Naur edited the ALGOL 60 report,

5
00:00:15,190 --> 00:00:18,110
is the N in BNF, and won the Turing Award.

6
00:00:18,390 --> 00:00:20,710
His essay, Programming as Theory Building,

7
00:00:20,730 --> 00:00:22,130
rewards a slower look.

8
00:00:22,630 --> 00:00:25,750
He contrasts two views. In the production view,

9
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:28,550
programming produces a program and some other texts.

10
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:31,970
In the theory building view, programmers form an insight,

11
00:00:31,990 --> 00:00:35,570
a theory, of the matters at hand. The texts are secondary.

12
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:37,720
Programming, for Naur,

13
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:40,000
is matching part of a real-world activity

14
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:42,340
to what a computer can do with symbols.

15
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:44,560
And since the world keeps changing,

16
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:47,260
it includes modifying the program to keep up.

17
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:50,140
A team extended another group's compiler,

18
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:51,840
with full documentation,

19
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:54,700
annotated code, and advice from its authors.

20
00:00:55,070 --> 00:00:57,990
Still, several of their proposals were patches that

21
00:00:58,010 --> 00:01:00,230
would have destroyed its power and simplicity.

22
00:01:00,790 --> 00:01:02,370
The authors saw it instantly.

23
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:06,220
Ten years on, without them, the structure was still visible,

24
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:08,700
but made ineffective by amorphous additions.

25
00:01:09,270 --> 00:01:13,110
Second case: a two-hundred-thousand-line monitoring system.

26
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:16,310
The programmers who'd been there since its design fixed

27
00:01:16,330 --> 00:01:19,430
faults from what they knew and the annotated code,

28
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:21,070
and couldn't imagine any further

29
00:01:21,090 --> 00:01:22,850
documentation that would help.

30
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:25,580
Teams with full manuals kept getting stuck

31
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:28,080
on problems the veterans cleared up easily.

32
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:29,990
For the kind of knowledge,

33
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:32,650
Naur turns to the philosopher Gilbert Ryle.

34
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:35,990
Intelligent behavior, like telling jokes or fishing,

35
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:39,150
means doing things well and catching your own lapses.

36
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:40,720
It isn't rule-following,

37
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:42,960
or you'd need rules for applying the rules,

38
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:44,160
and so on forever.

39
00:01:44,490 --> 00:01:47,790
Having a theory goes further: you can explain what you do,

40
00:01:47,810 --> 00:01:49,870
answer questions, and argue about it.

41
00:01:50,310 --> 00:01:52,050
A theory isn't just its laws.

42
00:01:52,490 --> 00:01:54,330
Having Newton's theory means seeing how

43
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:56,510
it applies to pendulums and planets,

44
00:01:56,570 --> 00:01:58,490
and recognizing similar cases.

45
00:01:58,870 --> 00:02:01,710
That kind of similarity can't be put into rules,

46
00:02:01,770 --> 00:02:04,390
any more than the likeness of faces or tunes.

47
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:06,970
So a theory can't be fully written down.

48
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:09,670
A program's theory is how certain affairs

49
00:02:09,690 --> 00:02:11,710
of the world will be handled by it.

50
00:02:11,930 --> 00:02:14,830
Its holder can do three things no document can.

51
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:17,560
Explain how each part maps to the world,

52
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:20,020
including which parts of the world matter at all.

53
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:22,760
Justify why each part is as it is.

54
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:24,960
And meet a request for change by seeing

55
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:26,900
how it resembles what's already there.

56
00:02:27,370 --> 00:02:30,570
We expect change to be cheap because a program is text,

57
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:32,090
and text is easy to edit.

58
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:34,650
Naur calls that argument false:

59
00:02:35,150 --> 00:02:36,670
text was never the main cost.

60
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:40,160
Altering a building can cost more than rebuilding it.

61
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:42,860
And built-in flexibility costs money now,

62
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,600
for needs that may never come.

63
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:47,820
Any change can be made many ways, all correct.

64
00:02:48,140 --> 00:02:50,740
Some extend the theory. Others are patches.

65
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,180
Only someone with the theory can tell which.

66
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,880
That's how programs decay.

67
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:58,620
Even simplicity and good structure only make sense against

68
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:01,340
the programs that could have been written instead.

69
00:03:01,910 --> 00:03:03,950
So a program is alive while a team

70
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:06,230
holding its theory controls its changes.

71
00:03:06,570 --> 00:03:09,990
It dies when that team dissolves, even if it keeps running.

72
00:03:10,410 --> 00:03:11,970
The death shows when requests for

73
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:14,490
change can't be answered intelligently.

74
00:03:15,030 --> 00:03:18,130
Rebuilding the theory from code and documents alone is,

75
00:03:18,310 --> 00:03:20,490
Naur says, strictly impossible.

76
00:03:21,010 --> 00:03:23,630
He'd rather a new team solved the problem afresh.

77
00:03:23,930 --> 00:03:26,130
A theory is passed on the way music is taught:

78
00:03:26,150 --> 00:03:28,830
by working closely with people who have it.

79
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:30,920
Can a method stand in for it?

80
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:32,720
A method is a set of work rules:

81
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:35,200
steps, order, notations, documents.

82
00:03:35,630 --> 00:03:38,330
But a theory has no inherent parts or order.

83
00:03:38,590 --> 00:03:40,070
So there is no right method,

84
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:41,790
though methods help as education.

85
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:44,300
And programmers aren't replaceable

86
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:46,100
parts on a production line.

87
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:47,480
They're responsible,

88
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:48,920
permanent developers of the

89
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:51,200
activity the computer is part of,

90
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:53,540
with the standing of engineers and lawyers.

91
00:03:54,210 --> 00:03:57,770
Now agents write the code, and text really is nearly free.

92
00:03:58,130 --> 00:04:00,630
On Naur's argument, the cost hasn't gone.

93
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:02,390
It has moved to specifying.

94
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:06,060
An agent dropped into a codebase with only code and

95
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:09,480
documents is attempting what Naur called revival.

96
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:12,600
Its picture of the system can differ from the original.

97
00:04:12,970 --> 00:04:14,490
So the spec is the handoff,

98
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:16,750
and writing it is where the theory gets built.

99
00:04:17,010 --> 00:04:19,710
The agent gets the spec. You keep the theory.

100
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:21,840
In a regulated enterprise,

101
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:25,380
that theory is scattered: a rule buried in policy,

102
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:27,840
a platform quirk one engineer knows,

103
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:30,040
a constraint nobody says out loud.

104
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:32,640
Requirements gathering is theory building.

105
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:35,080
But if each spec starts from scratch,

106
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:38,580
each team rediscovers the same rules, quirks and owners.

107
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:41,860
Longer documents won't fix that. A loop will.

108
00:04:42,390 --> 00:04:44,690
Each piece of product shaping runs a loop:

109
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:48,410
discover what's true, verify it with the people who own it,

110
00:04:48,430 --> 00:04:50,310
specify, build with agents,

111
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:52,190
and learn from what building reveals.

112
00:04:52,630 --> 00:04:55,330
Then it writes back what it learned, as governed facts.

113
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:57,880
A governed fact cites its source,

114
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:00,840
names an owner who vouches for it, carries a date,

115
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:03,540
and says whether it's verified or still assumed.

116
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:05,600
Decisions keep their reasons.

117
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:08,150
The next loop starts from those facts.

118
00:05:08,470 --> 00:05:11,010
An adjacent feature inherits most of them.

119
00:05:11,250 --> 00:05:13,830
An unrelated one still reuses the rules,

120
00:05:14,050 --> 00:05:15,830
platforms and people they share.

121
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:20,060
Each loop has fewer unknowns to resolve, so it moves faster,

122
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:22,620
and what's left is the work that needs judgment.

123
00:05:22,940 --> 00:05:24,300
And when a rule changes,

124
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:26,720
you can see every spec that relied on it.

125
00:05:27,150 --> 00:05:29,390
This works with Naur, not around him.

126
00:05:29,890 --> 00:05:32,230
Facts aren't the theory, but they're what it's built on.

127
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:35,160
And the loop keeps the people who hold the theory in it,

128
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:36,620
verifying and deciding.

129
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,820
That's what keeps a product alive.

130
00:05:39,450 --> 00:05:42,050
So: trace every requirement to a governed fact.

131
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:44,420
Write down the why, and what you rejected.

132
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:47,860
Rehearse change: does a new rule fit, or need a patch?

133
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,480
Close each loop by writing back what you learned.

134
00:05:50,760 --> 00:05:53,760
And keep the theory's holders in the loop as agents build.

135
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:57,250
Code is the output. The spec is the handoff.

136
00:05:57,690 --> 00:06:00,650
Governed facts are the memory. The theory is the product.
